First, note that \(\sqrt{18} = \sqrt{9 \times 2} = 3\sqrt{2}\), so \(\sqrt{18}\) is already in \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\).

Therefore, the field extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}, \sqrt{18})\) simplifies to \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\).

The degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\) is 2, and adjoining \(\sqrt{3}\) (which is not in \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\)) doubles the degree again.

Hence, the degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\) is \(2 \times 2 = 4\).

<solution>4</solution>
